From: cerXXXX <polyakovia2009@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 21:51:52 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] fix: don't let mpv sleep inside the render loop

mpv_render_context_render () defaults to MPV_RENDER_PARAM_BLOCK_FOR_TARGET_TIME
= 1 and sleeps until the next video frame is due. The render loop already
paces itself (frame callback plus the --fps sleep), so the two waits stack up
and, depending on how the video's frame times fall against that pacing, a
video wallpaper can update far below its frame rate.

1080p24 H.264 wallpaper on niri: 13 rendered frames/s before, 23.4 after
(counted from wl_surface.attach); playback speed checked against a video with
a burned-in clock.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/WallpaperEngine/VideoPlayback/MPV/GLPlayer.cpp | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/WallpaperEngine/VideoPlayback/MPV/GLPlayer.cpp b/src/WallpaperEngine/VideoPlayback/MPV/GLPlayer.cpp
index 39f1ff8..f0e674a 100644
--- a/src/WallpaperEngine/VideoPlayback/MPV/GLPlayer.cpp
+++ b/src/WallpaperEngine/VideoPlayback/MPV/GLPlayer.cpp
@@ -167,9 +167,13 @@ void GLPlayer::render () const {
 
     // no need to flip as it'll be handled by the wallpaper rendering code
     int flip_y = 0;
+    // the render loop already paces frames; letting mpv also sleep until the next video
+    // frame's display time stacks both waits and roughly halves the video's frame rate
+    int block_for_target_time = 0;
 
     mpv_render_param params[] = { { MPV_RENDER_PARAM_OPENGL_FBO, &fbo },
 				  { MPV_RENDER_PARAM_FLIP_Y, &flip_y },
+				  { MPV_RENDER_PARAM_BLOCK_FOR_TARGET_TIME, &block_for_target_time },
 				  { MPV_RENDER_PARAM_INVALID, nullptr } };
 
     mpv_render_context_render (this->m_renderContext, params);
